  "question": {
    "text": "Let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>P</mi></math> be a point on the ellipse <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mn>9</mn></mfrac><mo>+</mo><mfrac><msup><mi>y</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mn>4</mn></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></math>. Let the line passing through <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>P</mi></math> and parallel to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>y</mi><mo>-</mo></math>axis meet the circle <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>9</mn></math> at point <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>Q</mi></math> such that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>P</mi><mo> </mo><mtext>and</mtext><mo> </mo><mi>Q</mi></math> are on the same side of the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>x</mi><mo>-</mo></math>axis. Then, the eccentricity of the locus of the point <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>R</mi></math> on <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>P</mi><mi>Q</mi></math> such that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>P</mi><mi>R</mi><mo>:</mo><mi>R</mi><mi>Q</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>:</mo><mn>3</mn></math> as <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>P</mi></math> moves on the ellipse, is:",
